# Line endings.
#
# The published leetenum.sh carried one CR per line — 566 of them — which made
# bash execute lines 2 and 6 as bogus $'\r' commands and then abort at the first
# function definition. No function was ever defined, so no argument parsing ever
# ran: the only published interface was dead on Linux, macOS and WSL alike.
#
# `* text=auto` normalises on the way into the index. The later, more specific
# rules pin the files where a CR is not a cosmetic problem but a syntax error:
# a shell interpreter treats \r as part of the token, and a Go raw string or a
# testdata fixture compares byte-for-byte.
#
# Order matters here — the last matching pattern wins.
* text=auto

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.go    text eol=lf
*.mod   text eol=lf
*.sum   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.rb    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf

# Extensionless executables. Matched by name because there is nothing else to
# match on, and these are the files where a stray CR is fatal.
leetenum        text eol=lf
install.sh      text eol=lf

# Binary, or otherwise not to be touched.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tgz   binary
*.zip   binary
*.tar   binary

# Wordlists and resolver lists ship as data and must stay byte-identical, so the
# shell and Go implementations agree on the permutation output the tests compare.
*.txt   -text
